Motion & Microinteractions
Animation is communication. Every motion in Context has a purpose — guiding attention, confirming actions, or creating emotional resonance.
PHASE 10
Motion as a Design Tool
In a learning app, motion isn't decoration. It's pedagogy. A word card that flips teaches object permanence. A progress bar that fills teaches accumulation. A streak flame that dances teaches reward. Motion makes the invisible visible.
MOTION PRINCIPLES
Animation Guidelines
Purposeful
Responsive
Consistent
Accessible
Every animation answers "why does this move?" If no clear answer, remove it. Motion for decoration erodes trust over time.
Touch → visual response in <100ms. Result animations complete in 200-400ms. No animation blocks user input.
Same easing curves across the app. Ease-out for entries, ease-in for exits, spring for interactive feedback. One physics model.
All animations respect prefers-reduced-motion. Essential state changes work without animation. Duration never gates functionality.
KEY MICROINTERACTIONS
Signature Motion Moments
Correct Answer
Wrong Answer
Streak Milestone
Level Completion
Green pulse ripple from the answer, card lifts slightly, checkmark draws on. Haptic tap. Total: 350ms. Feels earned.
Gentle horizontal shake (not aggressive), amber glow, subtle nudge toward correct answer. Haptic buzz. Total: 250ms. Feels safe.
Flame icon scales up, particles burst, counter increments with spring animation. Number odometer effect. Total: 600ms. Feels celebratory.
Full-screen confetti (restrained, not overwhelming), XP counter animates up, new world unlocks with door-opening transition. Total: 1.2s.
DELIVERABLES
What This Phase Produces
Motion Specification Document
Interaction Prototypes
Haptic Design Map
Every animation documented: trigger, duration, easing, spring constants, delay. Exportable to Lottie or CSS.
Figma prototypes demonstrating each key interaction flow. Clickable, testable, shareable.
When and how haptic feedback is used. Light tap for selections, medium for confirmations, pattern for achievements.
